  {
    "article_index": 2,
    "text": "The Mars Volta finally release an \"acoustic\" album, \"Octahedron.\"  This effort is perhaps an attempt to appease the fans and the critics who stood dejectedly, with open mouths, scratching their heads over their past three albums.  I enjoyed their past three albums (\"Frances the Mute,\" \"Amputechture,\" and \"The Bedlam in Goliath\").  The Mars Volta are intense and strangely different.  (We need this in an industry of soulless hacks and pop-singers pretending to be rock `n' rollers.)  \"Octahedron\" is another mind-blowing experience (yeah, yeah...I know, everybody says that about progressive rock, but let's ride the clich together on this one).  I'm happy these boys have the gusto to challenge their collective listeners.  I enjoyed all eight tracks.  This album really \"hangs together.\"  I love listening to an album without the urge to skip tracks.  The opener, \"Since We've Been Wrong,\" had me thinking, \"They love to surprise their listeners.\"  I've had the closer, \"Desperate Graves,\" in my head since I heard it.  \"Halo of Nembutals\" and \"Cotopaxi\" are my favorite tracks.  From the falsetto vocals to the innovative guitar work, each track is inspired and unique.  Volta albums tend to feel bloated with noise; this album is lean (and still mean).  In short, I enjoyed all 50 minutes.  Hopefully the fans will unite under \"Octahedron.\"  The Mars Volta deliver the goods.  This is rock music for rock `n' rollers.",
