  {
    "article_index": 8,
    "text": "We have owned 3 of these, now, with two dogs.  We bought them based on the recommendations of the trainers in our dog school.  First, they said that these push/pull on the chest instead of pulling from the throat as other harnesses do.  But, any properly fit harness does not pull at the throat.  Next, the plastic snaps they use for fastening would not click together and we were constantly fiddling with getting them to snap together.  The adjustments to get a proper fit were frustrating and took forever.  Thankfully, one of our dogs was very patient while we tried to fit it!.  But our biggest complaint was how they function on real walks.  I noticed pictures always show the leash ahead of or to the side of the dog.  What we found was that if you walk your dog tight (next to you) so that you could keep the leash up, they worked o.k.  If you allowed the dog to walk ahead on leash, they are terrible.  Because of where the leash snaps in front, if your dog is walking ahead of you the leash wants to drop down in front of their foot.  Inevitably, it will work under their foot and body and then you are stopping to free the leash and bring it back into a relaxed position next to the dog.  So, if you are always ahead of or next to your dog, or have a big dog where you can walk holding the least up (try that for a mile), and if you don't mind the frustration of fastening and adjusting it, then this leash is for you.  We didn't like it. Oh, and I noticed other reviewers complained about it rubbing on the pits of the dogs --- we found that, too, and our trainer even sized the halter.  If your leash has a metal hook and you connect it to the front ring and the dog walks forward, it also seems to rub/bang on their front leg.  We do not recommend it.",
